<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="pre" data-label="Where do I stand?">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">Before we begin</span> Where do I stand?</div>
  <h2>Two questions, not one</h2>
  <p>People collapse them constantly, and the collapse is the source of most bad conversations about faith.</p>
  <p>The first is about <b>belief</b>: do you hold that deity exists? The second is about <b>knowledge</b>: do you claim to <em>know</em> it—and can you say what warrants the claim?</p>
  <p>These are independent. A person can believe firmly and claim no certainty. A person can disbelieve and freely admit they cannot prove it. And knowing is more than believing hard; it is believing with a warrant you can point to. Section 1 asks what counts as one.</p>
  <p class="ab-big">Place yourself where you actually are.</p>
  <p>Not where you think you should be. Not where you would defend yourself if pressed.</p>

  <p>This map does not measure what happens to you. It measures your present state, your intentions, and your leanings.</p>
