  <p class="ab-hint" id="ab-hintA"><span id="ab-hintAmsg">Click anywhere on the grid. The edges are the hard forms of each position; the center is the soft.</span> The vertical axis is degree of belief—what you hold to be true. The horizontal axis is knowledge—the degree to which you claim to know it, and can give warrant for the claim.</p>
  <p><em>Deity</em> here means one or more. Christians themselves differ on how the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit are one. This map does not decide that question, and your placement should not have to either.</p>
  <p>If you have never truly asked either question, you are not on this map yet. That is not a failing. It is a legitimate place to start, and this resource was written for you as much as for anyone.</p>

  <div class="ab-result" id="ab-resA">
    <h3 id="ab-nameA"></h3><p id="ab-defA"></p>
    <div class="ab-approach" id="ab-appA"></div>
    <p class="ab-q">Now—can you say <em>why</em>?</p>
    <p style="font-size:.94rem;color:var(--inkMute)">Not why someone else holds your position. Why <em>you</em> do. If the answer comes quickly and in someone else's words, that is worth noticing.</p>
    <div class="ab-personal">
      <textarea data-j="pre-why" placeholder="I hold this because…"></textarea>
    </div>
    <p>And one harder question, which I used to put to laboratory students: <b>how do you know what you don't know, when you don't know that you don't know it?</b></p>
    <p class="ab-studyonly">You can't—not alone. You need a witness outside your own faculties: another mind arguing honestly against you, or a faculty the ones you already trust do not include. The next four sections supply the first. Section 1 proposes the second.</p>
    <small>Plato, <em>Meno</em> 80d.</small>
  </div>
  <div class="ab-notes"><h4>Facilitator</h4><ul>
    <li>Do not let anyone answer for someone else. Silence here is productive.</li>
    <li>Ask aloud: <em>what would have to be true for you to move one box in any direction?</em></li>
    <li>Watch for people who place themselves where they think they <em>ought</em> to be. Name it gently.</li>
    <li>Ask the twisty question and wait. Do not answer it. Collect two or three attempts, then advance.</li>
  </ul></div>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="pre" data-label="Working definitions">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">Before we begin</span> Where do I stand?</div>
  <h3>Four words, used carefully</h3>
  <p><b>Theist.</b> Believes that deity exists—one or more.</p>
  <p><b>Atheist.</b> Does not believe that deity exists. This is a judgment, not an absence; Section 3 argues it by its own best reasons.</p>
  <p><b>Agnostic.</b> Does not claim to know. In its <em>soft</em> form: <em>I</em> do not know. In its <em>hard</em> form, Huxley's own: <em>no one</em> can know. Both live on the left half of the map.</p>
  <p><b>Gnostic.</b> Claims to know. In its <em>soft</em> form: I know, and I know that my knowing is partial. In its <em>hard</em> form: I know fully and certainly. Both live on the right half.</p>
  <p>Notice what none of the four names: ignorance itself. Every position on this map has gaps. The map does not measure whether you have them. It measures how honest you are about them.</p>
</article>

<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="1" data-label="How We Come to Know">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-02.jpg" alt="Four antique brass keys of different shapes, laid side by side.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">1</span> How We Come to Know</div>
  <h2>Before we compare answers, let us agree on what counts as knowing</h2>
  <p>Most arguments about God are actually arguments about evidence — conducted by people who never said out loud what they would accept as evidence. So let us do that part first.</p>
  <p>Human beings gather knowledge along four natural paths.</p>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>The Senses</b><span>What we perceive directly: seen, heard, touched, measured.<br><i>Establishes:</i> the physical world, reliably and repeatably.<br><i>Cannot reach:</i> anything leaving no physical trace — and cannot check its own accuracy without using itself.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>Authority</b><span>What we accept on the testimony of others: teachers, texts, institutions, experts.<br><i>Establishes:</i> nearly everything any of us knows. You have not personally verified the speed of light.<br><i>Cannot reach:</i> certainty beyond the reliability of the witness.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>Reason</b><span>What we derive by logic from what we already hold.<br><i>Establishes:</i> consistency, valid inference, the exposure of contradiction.<br><i>Cannot reach:</i> new premises. Reason processes; it does not originate.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>Academic Intuition</b><span>Conclusions arriving without conscious derivation, drawn from accumulated experience — the physicist who <em>sees</em> the answer before working it. A rational process, however mysterious it feels.<br><i>Establishes:</i> insight faster than deliberation.<br><i>Cannot reach:</i> past the experience it was built from.</span></div>
  <p>These four together are the <span class="ab-g" data-def="The four natural paths to knowledge: senses, authority, reason, and academic intuition.">natural framework</span>, and it is not defective. It functions precisely as designed for the questions it is equipped to address. Philosophy, the arts, and the sciences have yielded incalculable benefit to human life. Nothing here asks you to think less of them.</p>
  <div class="ab-notes"><h4>Facilitator</h4><ul>
    <li>Press the authority card. Most people underestimate how much of what they "know" is testimony.</li>
    <li>Good aside: <em>which of these four did you use to get to work this morning?</em> All four.</li>
  </ul></div>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="1" data-label="Three axioms">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-03.jpg" alt="Three smooth river stones stacked in balance.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">1</span> How We Come to Know</div>
  <h3>Three working axioms</h3>
  <p>To go further we need three assumptions. They cannot be proven — every framework rests on something unproven — but they are the ones science itself runs on.</p>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>1 · A shared reality</b><span>We exist together in the same world.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>2 · Genuine perception</b><span>To some extent we perceive that reality, and our own nature within it.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>3 · Sufficient uniformity</b><span>Reality is regular enough to be explored with some degree of certainty — through <em>patterns; cause and effect; scale, proportion, and quantity; systems and system models; energy and matter; structure and function;</em> and <em>stability and change</em>.</span></div>
  <p>Those seven are not improvised. They are the <b>crosscutting concepts</b> — the organizing principles the Next Generation Science Standards identify as the lenses through which every scientific discipline examines the natural world. I taught them in a public-school science laboratory for twenty-six years.</p>
  <p>They are how a scientist actually reasons about anything. And they work just as well pointed at questions science does not usually ask.</p>
  <small>NGSS Lead States, <em>Next Generation Science Standards: For States, By States</em>, Appendix G (2013).</small>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="1" data-label="What truth is">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-04.jpg" alt="A small hourglass, sand caught mid-fall.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">1</span> How We Come to Know</div>
  <h3>What truth is</h3>
  <p>Philosophy offers a long menu — <span class="ab-g" data-def="A world exists independently of our perception of it.">realism</span>, <span class="ab-g" data-def="Reality is fundamentally mental; the material depends on mind.">idealism</span>, <span class="ab-g" data-def="Knowledge derives primarily from sensory experience.">empiricism</span>, <span class="ab-g" data-def="Only physical things exist; consciousness reduces to physical process.">materialism</span>, <span class="ab-g" data-def="Knowledge is actively constructed rather than passively discovered.">constructivism</span>, <span class="ab-g" data-def="A belief's truth is measured by its practical consequences.">pragmatism</span>, <span class="ab-g" data-def="Truth is relative to culture, society, or historical context.">relativism</span>, <span class="ab-g" data-def="Individuals create meaning through freedom, choice, and action.">existentialism</span>, and more. Each names something real. None settles the question, and a reader can spend a lifetime in that menu without ever eating.</p>
  <p>Here is the definition this resource proceeds on:</p>
  <blockquote style="font-size:1.2rem">"Truth is knowledge of things as they are, and as they were, and as they are to come."<cite>{% scripture "Doctrine and Covenants 93:24" %}</cite></blockquote>
  <p>Examine it on its own terms. It makes truth a matter of <em>correspondence to actual states</em> — past, present, and future. It is not a claim about God; it is a claim about what the word <em>truth</em> means. You may hold it without holding anything else here.</p>
  <p>And notice what follows immediately. If truth spans what <em>was</em>, what <em>is</em>, and what <em>is to come</em> — and our natural faculties sample only a portion of that whole — then those faculties will reach a limit. Not because they are broken. Because of what they are.</p>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="1" data-label="The ceiling">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-05.jpg" alt="A wooden ladder leaning upward into pale cloud.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">1</span> How We Come to Know</div>
  <h3>The ceiling</h3>
  <p>Certain questions exceed the natural framework's reach:</p>
  <blockquote>Why is there something rather than nothing?<br>Does a loving God exist?<br>Does personal consciousness persist beyond death?<br>What is the ultimate purpose of my existence?</blockquote>
  <p>Science can describe <em>how</em> things work. It does not tell us <em>why</em> we are here — and it does not claim to.</p>
  <p class="ab-big">This is not a failure. The framework itself predicts the limit.</p>
  <p>Which leaves a genuine fork. You can accept permanent ignorance on the questions that matter most. Or you can ask whether there exists a faculty the four do not include.</p>
  <p>Words of Plainness proposes a fifth: <b>spiritual intuition</b> — the direct perception of truth by inspiration or revelation. It is sometimes called <em>irrational</em> intuition. No insult is intended by that word; <em>irrational</em> here means only that human reasoning is not its source.</p>
  <p>That is a claim, not a proof, and it is the claim everything downstream depends on. We will come back to how it can be tested.</p>
  <div class="ab-notes"><h4>Facilitator</h4><ul>
    <li>Strongest objection, and worth surfacing yourself: <em>is the ceiling real, or just the current edge of what we happen not to know yet?</em></li>
    <li>Let that sit unresolved. The resource is stronger for it.</li>
  </ul></div>
</article>

