<!-- ============ SECTION 2 · THEIST ============ -->
<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="2" data-label="Reasons to be a Theist">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">2</span> Compelling Reasons to be a Theist</div>
  <figure class="ab-fig ab-banner"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/fingers.jpg" alt="Detail of two hands reaching toward one another, nearly touching, in the manner of Michelangelo&#39;s Creation of Adam." style="object-position:center 42%"><figcaption>The reach toward contact — the theist claim in a single gesture.</figcaption></figure>
  <blockquote>"For since the creation of the world God's invisible qualities — his eternal power and divine nature — have been clearly seen, being understood from what has been made."<cite>{% scripture "Romans 1:20" %}</cite></blockquote>
  <p><b>Theism</b> — from the Greek <em>theos</em>, "god." At its broadest, the belief that at least one deity exists; more restrictively, belief in God as Creator together with an openness to revelation. It contains an enormous range: deism, monotheism, trinitarianism, henotheism, pantheism, and more.</p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Cosmological Argument.</b> Whatever begins to exist has a cause. The universe began to exist. Therefore it has a cause — one which, being the cause of space and time, must be outside both. <span class="ab-src">Aquinas, Summa Theologiae Ia, q. 2, a. 3; Leibniz; W.L. Craig, <em>The Kal&#257;m Cosmological Argument</em>, 1979.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Teleological Argument.</b> The ordered complexity of the universe — particularly the conditions required for life — is better explained by design than by chance. <span class="ab-src">Paley, Natural Theology, 1802.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Fine-Tuned Universe.</b> Several physical constants fall within extraordinarily narrow life-permitting ranges. Alter the cosmological constant or the strong nuclear force slightly and there is no chemistry, no stars, no observers. <span class="ab-src">Robin Collins, "The Teleological Argument: An Exploration of the Fine-Tuning of the Universe," in <em>The Blackwell Companion to Natural Theology</em>, 2009.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Moral Argument.</b> If objective moral obligations exist — if cruelty is genuinely wrong and not merely disfavored — they require a ground beyond human preference. <span class="ab-src">Lewis, Mere Christianity I; Adams, Finite and Infinite Goods, 1999.</span></p>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="2" data-label="Theist, continued">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-06.jpg" alt="The keystone at the top of a stone arch.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">2</span> Compelling Reasons to be a Theist</div>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Argument from Contingency.</b> Everything we observe might not have existed. A chain of contingent things, however long, does not explain itself. <span class="ab-src">Leibniz; Swinburne, The Existence of God, 2nd ed. 2004.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Historicity.</b> The resurrection is defended as a historical claim on the standard tools of ancient history: multiple early independent sources, the criterion of embarrassment, and the rise of a movement whose central claim was falsifiable in its own city within living memory. <span class="ab-src">N.T. Wright, The Resurrection of the Son of God, 2003.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Properly Basic Belief.</b> Belief in God may be rational without being inferred — as belief in other minds, or in the reliability of memory, is rational without proof. <span class="ab-src">Plantinga, Warranted Christian Belief, 2000.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Personal Experience.</b> Enormous numbers of people across every culture and century report encounter with the divine — experiences they describe as more certain than argument and frequently transformative.</p>
  <div class="ab-obj">
    <h4>What theism must answer</h4>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Problem of Evil.</b> Gratuitous suffering — the child's cancer, the earthquake, the long private cruelty no one witnesses — is difficult to reconcile with a God simultaneously all-powerful, all-knowing, and wholly good. This is the strongest argument against the position I hold, and no answer I have encountered dissolves it entirely. <span class="ab-src">Mackie, 1955; Rowe, 1979.</span></p>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>Divine Hiddenness.</b> If God desires relationship with every person, the existence of sincere, non-resistant seekers who find nothing requires explanation. <span class="ab-src">Schellenberg, 1993.</span></p>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Inference Gap.</b> Even granting the cosmological and teleological arguments in full, they deliver a first cause and a designer. The distance to a God with a name, a character, and a Son is considerable — and is not crossed by argument.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ab-notes"><h4>Facilitator</h4><ul>
    <li>Read the objections aloud yourself. A theist facilitator naming the problem of evil as unanswered buys enormous credibility.</li>
    <li>Do not resolve it here. Section 7 addresses the shape of the answer, not the answer.</li>
  </ul></div>
</article>

