<!-- ============ SECTION 3 · ATHEIST ============ -->
<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="3" data-label="Reasons to be an Atheist">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">3</span> Compelling Reasons to be an Atheist</div>
  <figure class="ab-fig ab-banner"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/atheism.jpg" alt="A dictionary page with the entry &#39;Atheism&#39; highlighted in pink marker, the definition beginning &#39;doctrine or belief that there is no God.&#39;"><figcaption>A definition, marked for examination — which is exactly how this section treats it.</figcaption></figure>
  <blockquote>"I had no need of that hypothesis."<cite>Pierre-Simon Laplace, reportedly, to Napoleon</cite></blockquote>
  <p><b>Atheism</b> — from <em>a-</em> and <em>theos</em>. Disbelief in, or denial of, the existence of divine beings. <b>Atheism is not a belief system</b>, and it is not the same as being irreligious. It is an active judgment, usually grounded in personal experience or philosophical argument, and there are many varieties of atheist.</p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Burden of Proof.</b> Extraordinary claims require proportionate evidence, and the burden falls on the one asserting. Bertrand Russell put it this way: if he claimed a china teapot were orbiting the sun somewhere between Earth and Mars, too small for any telescope to find, no one could disprove him &#8212; but nobody would think the claim deserved belief merely because it could not be refuted. Absent evidence, non-belief is the appropriate default, not a competing faith. <span class="ab-src">Bertrand Russell, "Is There a God?", 1952; Sagan, 1995.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Sufficient Natural Explanation.</b> Cosmology, evolutionary biology, and neuroscience account for the universe's origin, life's diversity, and the operations of mind without invoking an agent. <span class="ab-src">Hume, Dialogues, 1779; Dennett, 1995.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Problem of Evil, as positive argument.</b> Not merely a difficulty for theism but evidence against it: gratuitous suffering is precisely what a universe without a benevolent governor would look like. <span class="ab-src">Mackie, 1955; Rowe, 1979; Draper, 1989.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Religious Pluralism.</b> Thousands of mutually exclusive traditions each claim revelation, and each is overwhelmingly predicted by the claimant's birthplace. <span class="ab-src">Hume, "Of Miracles," <em>Philosophical Essays Concerning Human Understanding</em>, 1748, sec. X.</span></p>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="3" data-label="Atheist, continued">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-07.jpg" alt="A small white porcelain teapot alone against empty space.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">3</span> Compelling Reasons to be an Atheist</div>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Naturalistic Accounts of Belief.</b> Cognitive science offers explanations for why humans reliably generate god-beliefs — agency detection, theory of mind, in-group cohesion — that require no god. <span class="ab-src">Boyer, Religion Explained, 2001.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Textual and Historical Criticism.</b> Sacred texts show every mark of human composition, redaction, and cultural situation.</p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Moral Autonomy.</b> Ethics can be grounded in reason, empathy, and reciprocity without divine command — and the non-religious are not observably less ethical. <span class="ab-src">Kant, Groundwork, 1785; Foot, Natural Goodness, 2001.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Absence of Personal Experience.</b> Where believers cite encounter as evidence, many have sought sincerely and found nothing. That absence is data too.</p>
  <div class="ab-obj">
    <h4>What atheism must answer</h4>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>Grounding Objective Morality.</b> If moral facts are real rather than preferred, naturalism must say what makes them so. If they are not real, "cruelty is wrong" reduces to "cruelty is disfavored" — and few atheists live as though that is all it means. <span class="ab-src">Mackie conceded the difficulty, 1977.</span></p>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Brute-Fact Terminus.</b> "Why is there something rather than nothing?" is answered with a stop rather than an explanation. That may be correct. It is not obviously more satisfying than the alternative stop.</p>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>Consciousness.</b> Why physical processes are accompanied by subjective experience remains unexplained on any current account. Not an argument for God — a live limit on the claim that the natural story is complete. <span class="ab-src">Chalmers, 1995.</span></p>
  </div>
</article>

