<!-- ============ SECTION 4 · AGNOSTIC ============ -->
<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="4" data-label="Reasons to be Agnostic">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">4</span> Compelling Reasons to be Agnostic</div>
  <figure class="ab-fig ab-banner"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/agnosticism.jpg" alt="A magnifying glass held over printed text, bringing the word &#39;agnosticism&#39; into sharp focus while surrounding repetitions of the word remain blurred."><figcaption>Huxley&#39;s word was a method before it was a position — a way of looking, not a conclusion.</figcaption></figure>
  <blockquote>"Agnosticism, in fact, is not a creed, but a method… In matters of the intellect, follow your reason as far as it will take you, without regard to any other consideration… do not pretend that conclusions are certain which are not demonstrated or demonstrable."<cite>Thomas Henry Huxley, "Agnosticism," 1889</cite></blockquote>
  <p><b>Agnosticism</b> — coined by Huxley in 1869, by his own later account. Huxley's specific form is the hard one: ultimate truths of this kind are unknowable to <em>any</em> human, not merely unknown to the speaker. The soft form—<em>I do not know, and I will not pretend to</em>—is far more common, and most of the arguments below serve it just as well.</p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Inconclusive Evidence.</b> The arguments on both sides are serious and neither is decisive. Suspending judgment where evidence is genuinely balanced is the correct response, not cowardice. <span class="ab-src">Huxley, 1889; Clifford, 1877.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Limits of Human Cognition.</b> Our concepts were built for mid-sized objects at moderate speeds. There is no reason to assume they scale to ultimate reality. <span class="ab-src">Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, 1781; Nagel, 1974.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Unknowable in Principle.</b> The ultimate cause may be not merely undiscovered but permanently beyond the categories available to a finite mind. <span class="ab-src">Spencer, First Principles, 1862.</span></p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Religious Pluralism.</b> Committing to one tradition among thousands, all sincerely held, requires a confidence the evidence may not support.</p>
  <p class="ab-arg"><b>Intellectual Humility.</b> Declining to assert more than one has is a virtue, not an evasion.</p>
  <div class="ab-obj">
    <h4>What agnosticism must answer</h4>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>The Practical Impossibility of Suspension.</b> On this question you cannot actually abstain. You will live either as though your life is accountable to something beyond itself or as though it is not. Neutrality is available in theory and not in practice. <span class="ab-src">Pascal, <em>Pens&#233;es</em> &#167;233 (Brunschvicg) = &#167;418 (Lafuma); James, "The Will to Believe," 1896.</span></p>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>Self-Application.</b> "Ultimate truths are unknowable to any human" is itself a strong claim about ultimate truth. What grants access to <em>that</em>?</p>
    <p class="ab-arg"><b>Asymmetric Rigor.</b> The evidential standard applied to religious claims is rarely the standard applied to history, other minds, or moral facts. Applied evenly, very little would survive.</p>
  </div>
</article>

