<!-- ============ SECTION 5 · MEROGNOSTICISM ============ -->
<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="5" data-label="The Space Between">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">5</span> A Word for the Space Between</div>
  <h2>Look again at the horizontal axis</h2>
  <p>It offered you two options: you claim to know, or you hold that such things cannot be known. <b>Gnostic</b> or <b>agnostic</b>.</p>
  <p>Thomas Henry Huxley coined <em>agnostic</em> in 1869 from the Greek — <em>a-</em>, "not," plus <em>gnōstos</em>, "known." It was an honest word and it filled a real gap. But it left another gap open, and that gap is where a great many thoughtful people actually live.</p>
  <p>Paul named the condition long before Huxley named its denial. Writing to Corinth he said <em>ek merous gar ginōskomen</em> —</p>
  <blockquote style="font-size:1.25rem">"For we know in part."<cite>{% scripture "1 Corinthians 13:9" %}</cite></blockquote>
  <p>Not <em>we do not know</em>. Not <em>we know fully</em>. We know — really know — <b>in part</b>.</p>
  <p>There has never been an English word for that. So I made one, on Huxley's own pattern: <em>meros</em>, "part," plus <em>gnōstos</em>, "known."</p>
  
  <p class="ab-big">Merognostic. One who knows in part.</p>
  <figure class="ab-fig"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/merognosticism.jpg" alt="A painted allegory: a robed figure holding a single puzzle piece stands before a large, partly assembled puzzle suspended in warm light. The completed pieces show a council of philosophers in session, a galaxy and planets, an open book with an eye, a lantern and magnifying glass, a balance scale, a deeply rooted tree, and people teaching and helping one another. Several piece-shaped gaps remain open and unfilled. Loose pieces lie on the pavement at his feet, and a stair of light rises at the centre toward a distant star."><figcaption>Enough pieces to see the picture. Honest about the ones still missing. <br><span style="opacity:.8">Illustration generated with ChatGPT to the author&#39;s specification, August 2026.</span></figcaption></figure>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="5" data-label="What it is not">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-08.jpg" alt="A broken pottery shard with part of its painted pattern still visible.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">5</span> A Word for the Space Between</div>
  <h3>Set the four side by side</h3>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>Gnostic (hard)</b><span>Claims complete and certain knowledge.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>Agnostic (hard)</b><span>Claims that nothing can be known.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card"><b>Skeptic</b><span>Is not sure whether anything has been given.</span></div>
  <div class="ab-card ab-hl"><b>Merognostic</b><span>Holds what has genuinely been given, and declines to invent the rest.</span></div>
  <p>On the map, that is the soft-gnostic half of the knowledge axis. I needed a word for it because "gnostic" alone is usually heard as the hard form, and "agnostic" gives away the part I actually hold.</p>
  <p>Here is how I say it about myself:</p>
  <blockquote>I am a merognostic, not an agnostic. I know God is real. I know the Spirit has spoken to me. And I know, with the same honesty, that what I hold is a portion.</blockquote>
  <p>I offer the word; I do not assign it. It is a description of my own honesty, not a category for yours. If it names something you recognize, it is yours to use. If it does not, the grid is still perfectly good, and you are still perfectly welcome here.</p>
  <p><b>One discipline keeps the word honest:</b> it is a self-description of honest faith, never a box into which other souls may be sorted. The moment it becomes a tribe, it has betrayed the posture it names.</p>
  <div class="ab-deeper"><strong>Go deeper:</strong> <a href="/studies/merognosticism/">Merognosticism — A Plain Confession of Partial Knowing</a></div>
  <div class="ab-notes"><h4>Facilitator</h4><ul>
    <li>This is the section that most often lands. Give it room and do not rush to the next slide.</li>
    <li>Never say "so you're a merognostic." Let people claim it or leave it.</li>
  </ul></div>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide ab-act-slide" data-sec="5" data-label="Reflect · 5">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-15.jpg" alt="An open blank notebook.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">5</span> Reflection</div>
  <div class="ab-act">
    <span class="ab-tag">Check your thinking</span>
    <h3>Which posture is speaking?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:.96rem">"I have felt God answer me. I could not tell you what heaven looks like, and I will not pretend to."</p>
    <div class="ab-opts" data-quiz="s5" data-answer="c">
      <div class="ab-opt" data-k="a"><span class="ab-k">A</span><span>Gnostic</span></div>
      <div class="ab-opt" data-k="b"><span class="ab-k">B</span><span>Agnostic</span></div>
      <div class="ab-opt" data-k="c"><span class="ab-k">C</span><span>Merognostic</span></div>
