</article>

<!-- ============ SECTION 6 · THIRD AXIS ============ -->
<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="6" data-label="The Third Axis">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-09.jpg" alt="A single deep footprint pressed into wet earth.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">6</span> The Third Axis</div>
  <h2>A question the grid raises and cannot answer</h2>
  <p>Two axes give four boxes. But something is obviously missing:</p>
  <p class="ab-big">The grid says nothing about how a person lives.</p>
  <p>A gnostic theist may be cruel. An agnostic atheist may be generous past all obligation. The map locates conviction and says nothing about consequence — and consequence is what anyone actually encounters when they meet you.</p>
  <div class="ab-act">
    <span class="ab-tag">Before you advance</span>
    <h3>Propose a third axis</h3>
    <p class="ab-q">What would you measure? Justify it.</p>
    <div class="ab-personal"><textarea data-j="s6-zaxis" placeholder="My proposed z-axis would measure…"></textarea></div>
    <div class="ab-notes" style="display:none"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="ab-notes"><h4>Facilitator</h4><ul>
    <li>Collect two or three proposals aloud before advancing. Do not advance early — the reveal only works if they have committed first.</li>
  </ul></div>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="6" data-label="What the machine proposed">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">6</span> The Third Axis</div>
  <h3>What the machine proposed</h3>
  <p>In January 2025 I put this exact question to an AI — ChatGPT 4o Mini — and asked it to propose a z-axis for the exercise. It returned a spectrum of <b>the consequences of actions, viewed through the framework of ecological ethics</b>:</p>
  <blockquote>"When viewed through the lens of ecological ethics, the spectrum of good and evil is measured by the impact of beliefs and practices on both sustainability of community and quality of life."<cite>ChatGPT 4o Mini, 1 January 2025</cite></blockquote>
  <figure class="ab-fig ab-plate"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/zaxis.png" alt="A three-dimensional coordinate diagram. The horizontal x-axis runs from agnostic to gnostic, the vertical y-axis from atheistic to theistic, and a third z-axis runs diagonally from good to evil, each axis carrying a translucent plane. A heading reads: Ecological Definition of Good and Evil."><figcaption>The exercise in three dimensions. X: knowledge · Y: belief · Z: consequences of actions.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="ab-fig ab-duo"><div class="ab-frame"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/trees.jpg" alt="A single tree split down the middle: the left half in full green leaf above a field of wildflowers in warm light, the right half bare and dead above cracked barren ground under storm cloud. A working definition of good is set over the living side and a working definition of evil over the barren side."><div class="ab-ov ab-left"><b>Working Definition</b><p><q>GOOD</q> results in interconnectedness, ethical stewardship, and holistic well-being.</p><p><q>GOOD</q> creates stability within communities.</p></div><div class="ab-ov ab-right"><b>Working Definition</b><p><q>EVIL</q> results in disconnection, exploitation, and neglected quality of life.</p><p><q>EVIL</q> destroys stability within communities.</p></div></div><figcaption>One tree, two trajectories. The framework reads the ground, not the root.</figcaption></figure>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="6" data-label="Evaluating it">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-17.jpg" alt="A brass plumb bob hanging still on a knotted cord.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">6</span> The Third Axis</div>
  <h3>What I think of that answer</h3>
  <p>I want to say plainly why I kept this, because "an AI suggested it" is not a reason to keep anything.</p>
  <p>I kept it because I taught it. For twenty-six years in a science laboratory I taught students to evaluate any system — a watershed, a cell, a food web, a population — by asking two questions: <em>does it sustain itself over time, and does it sustain the things that depend on it?</em> A system that consumes its own foundation is failing, however impressive its output looks in a given season. That is not a moral opinion. It is what the data does.</p>
  <p>The machine, without being told any of that, reached for the same instrument.</p>
  <h4>What it gets right</h4>
  <p>It is <b>measurable</b>, or at least observable over time. It is <b>available to everyone in the room</b> — a committed atheist and a committed believer can both use it honestly without either conceding anything. And it lands on something scripture says constantly: <em>by their fruits ye shall know them</em> ({% scripture "Matthew 7:20" %}). The test of a thing is what it produces. That is an ecological claim before it is a theological one.</p>
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico ab-left" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-16.jpg" alt="A weathered wooden ship's rudder.">
    <h4>How to hold it</h4>
  <p>This diagram does not measure what happens to you, but how you respond to your existence. Loss, disaster, and temptation arrive from outside your lean; the axis reads only how you meet them. Read it at whatever scope you are examining—your own mind and body, a marriage, a business, your relationship with God—and read it on yourself. Pointed at a neighbor it only reads the wake he leaves, and reads it late.</p>
  <p>Harm done in ignorance is still harm; the watershed does not ask what you meant. But <em>evil</em> on this axis is a lean, not an accident, and wisdom is largely the work of moving your accidental consequences into the column of things you chose.</p>
  <h4>Where it reads late</h4>
  <p>Two things stay upstream of everything this instrument knows how to measure. Another person's heart—which is why the axis is for self-location, not sorting. And your own turn. A life trending one direction can reverse, actually and not gradually, and the reversal is a change in what the person <em>is</em> before it is a change in anything the ground can show.</p>
  <p class="ab-big">A good instrument pointed at the wrong depth. It measures the wake a life leaves behind. It cannot see the thing steering.</p>
  <p>And notice—this is the same finding we reached about the natural framework in Section 1. A tool that works well within its domain, reaching a ceiling its own design predicts.</p>
  <div class="ab-notes"><h4>Facilitator</h4><ul>
    <li>A promise creates a duty that outlasts the arithmetic. Ask the room whether the axis can see that.</li>
  </ul></div>
</article>

