<!-- ============ SECTION 7 · REASON AND REVELATION ============ -->
<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="7" data-label="Reason and Revelation">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-18.jpg" alt="A wooden dovetail joint, its two cut pieces fitted together.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">7</span> Reason and Revelation</div>
  <h2>Two halves of a whole</h2>
  <p>Rational and critical thinking are <b>not</b> evil. Yet scripture asks something that sounds frightening to the careful mind: <em>"Trust in the Lord with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding"</em> ({% scripture "Proverbs 3:5-6" %}).</p>
  <p>The question is not whether to trust reason or to trust God. It is what each faculty is <em>for</em>.</p>
  <p>Reason is not the enemy of faith, and scripture nowhere says it is. When Isaiah records the Lord's invitation — <em>"Come now, and let us reason together"</em> — the reasoning is not a concession God makes to skeptics. It is the form the invitation takes.</p>
  <p class="ab-big">If any conflict exists between academic knowledge and spiritual knowledge, it is the result of a lack of sufficient information within one or both domains.</p>
  <p>That is a strong claim, and it is meant to be. It says the appearance of conflict is a reliable signal that something is not yet known — not a verdict that one side must lose.</p>
</article>

