  <p>Reason prepares the ground: it frames the question honestly, tests claims for coherence, guards against credulity, and interprets what revelation gives. Revelation answers what reason cannot reach, and hands reason new territory to work — because a revealed truth still has to be understood, integrated, and lived.</p>
</article>

<article class="ab-slide" data-sec="7" data-label="How Peter came to know">
  <img loading="lazy" class="ab-ico" src="/assets/studies/approaches-to-belief/icons/atb-11.jpg" alt="A fisherman's net draped over a wooden post to dry.">
  <div class="ab-eyebrow"><span class="ab-num">7</span> Reason and Revelation</div>
  <h3>How Peter came to know</h3>
  <p>"Lean not unto thine own understanding" is not an instruction to stop thinking. It is an instruction to stop asking one faculty to do work it was never built to do — and to stop concluding, when it fails, that the question has no answer.</p>
  <p>Jesus asked Peter directly: <em>"Whom say ye that I am?"</em></p>
  <p>Peter answered, <em>"Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God."</em></p>
  <p>And Jesus told him where that knowledge had come from: <em>"Flesh and blood hath not revealed it unto thee, but my Father which is in heaven"</em> ({% scripture "Matthew 16:15-17" %}).</p>
  <p>Peter had walked with Him, watched Him, listened to Him — every natural faculty engaged and fully employed.</p>
  <p class="ab-big">They brought him to the question. They did not produce the answer.</p>
  <p>One honest difficulty remains, and it deserves to be named rather than managed. Spiritual knowledge cannot be transferred the way a proof can. No one can hand you their witness. This is not special pleading — the same is true of every genuinely first-person knowledge, and no one doubts the reality of grief or love on the grounds that neither can be demonstrated to a stranger. But it does mean the invitation must take a particular form.</p>
  <p class="ab-lede">It cannot be <em>accept this</em>. It can only be <em>test this</em>.</p>
  <div class="ab-song">
    <h4>Two Halves of a Whole</h4>
    <small>An original song by Aaron Powner · Chapter 3 testimony</small>
